e reached through normal calls, choose another feasible category-specific area.\n- Keep one primary testing purpose.\n- Do not combine independent scenarios in one test.\n- Do not add unrelated assertions only to cover more code.\n\nPriorities:\n1. Correctness and pytest validity.\n2. Real reachable behavior only.\n3. No regression of accepted tests.\n4. Improvement of the measured score for the requested category.\n5. A genuinely new accepted rule in the requested category.\n6. Coverage of a meaningful area not already covered in that category.\n7. Simplicity.\n\nCategory meaning:\nFunctional testing checks the externally visible behavior of the analyzed function.\nUse the provided source code as the available behavioral specification.\nA functional test is about what the caller can observe, not about how the code internally reaches that result.\n\nFunctional rule abstraction vocabulary:\nUse terms such as observable behavior, input class, valid input class, invalid input class, output class, validation behavior, validation exception, boundary effect, accepted range, rejected range, threshold value, zero value, empty input, normal outcome, special outcome, rejected outcome, cause effect relation, required cause, exclusive cause, inclusive cause, masked effect, observable precedence, and final visible result.\n\nFunctional search areas:\n- Equivalence class: test one representative value from a meaningful valid or invalid input class.\n- Output class: choose inputs that produce a distinct returned result or exception.\n- Validation behavior: test a value that is visibly accepted or rejected by the function.\n- Boundary effect: test a value at, just inside, or just outside a behavior-changing boundary.\n- Empty or neutral input: test an empty, missing, or neutral input form when it has a visible result.\n- Category alternative: test one meaningful alternative of an input property.\n- Feasible combination: test a meaningful combination of input properties that changes the visible result.\n- Cause effect relation: test input conditions that jointly produce a specific visible effect.\n- Required cause: test a case where one condition matters only when another condition is also present.\n- Exclusive or inclusive cause: test a visible effect produced by one selected cause or by at least one alternative cause.\n- Masked effect: test a case where one visible outcome prevents another possible outcome.\n- Observable precedence: test a case where the final visible result is determined by the priority of rules.\n- Normal outcome: test a typical accepted case.\n- Special outcome: test a non-default visible result.\n- Type or domain rejection: test invalid type or invalid value behavior only when it is supported by the code.\n- Collection or membership effect: test a collection size, presence, or absence case that changes the visible result.\n- State-like input effect: test a boolean or mode-like input that changes the visible result.\n\nFunctional novelty:\nA new functional rule must add a different observable behavior, validation behavior, boundary effect, output class, cause effect relation, or observable precedence relation.\nChanging only concrete values is not enough if the same visible behavior has already been tested.\n\nReturn exactly one complete concrete Python pytest test function and nothing else.\nDo not write import statements.\nDo not write markdown.\nDo not write explanations outside the function.\nImportant for this request:\n- Propose a genuinely new rule in this category.\n- The new rule must be genuinely distinct from the existing explicit rules shown below.\n- Do not repeat a previously rejected idea.\n- Do not make only a superficial variation of a rejected idea.\n- Do not make only a narrower restatement, cosmetic variation, or concrete specialization of an existing explicit rule.\n- Prefer a different observable behavior, branch outcome, condition outcome, loop behavior, or execution path from the already accepted tests, existing explicit rules, and rejected attempts.\n- Prefer a test that changes the measured score for this category.\n\nCategory: functional\nExisting explicit bullets in this category: 1\nNext bullet number if accepted: 2\n\nExisting explicit rules in this category:\n1. test a valid input case that should return the normal accepted result\n\nCurrent accepted tests for this category:\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom to_test import *\n\n# Sfarsitul implementarii testelor initiale existente.\n\n```\n\nSource code to test:\n```python\n# This function analyzes a simple order and returns a status string.\n# It first validates that all numeric inputs are non-negative.\n# Then it performs a consistency check by counting the number of items through a loop\n# and comparing the counted value with the original `items` value.\n# After validation and consistency checking, it classifies the order:\n# - minors with totals greater than 100 require manual review;\n# - all other valid orders are accepted;\n# - orders with zero items are marked as empty, overriding the previous status.\n\ndef analyze_orders(age: int, items: int, total: float) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a simple status for an order.\n\n    Rules:\n    - age must be >= 0\n    - items must be >= 0\n    - total must be >= 0\n    - the function also performs a small consistency check by counting the items\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Validate the basic input domain.\n    # This branch is an important test target because any negative parameter\n    # must stop the function immediately by raising a ValueError.\n    if age < 0 or items < 0 or total < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Parametrii nu pot fi negativi.\")\n\n    # Count the items manually in order to create a simple internal consistency check.\n    # This loop is a useful test target because mutations may change the range,\n    # the increment, or the final counted value.\n    counted_items = 0\n    for _ in range(items):\n        counted_items += 1\n\n    # Verify that the manual count matches the original number of items.\n    # In normal execution this should always be true, but the branch documents\n    # the intended defensive behavior for an inconsistent internal state.\n    if counted_items != items:\n        return \"internal_error\"\n\n    # Apply the main business rule:\n    # an order made by a minor with a total greater than 100 requires review.\n    # This condition is an important target for boundary and mutation tests.\n    if age < 18 and total > 100:\n        status = \"needs_review\"\n    else:\n        status = \"accepted\"\n\n    # Override the previous status when the order contains no items.\n    # This branch is important because it changes the result even if the order\n    # was already classified as accepted or as needing review.\n    if items == 0:\n        status = \"empty_order\"\n\n    # Return the final classification after all validation and rule checks.\n    return status\n```", "response": "```python\ndef test_boundary_effect_age_and_total():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        analyze_orders(age=-1, items=5, total=200)\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        analyze_orders(age=17, items=5, total=-1)\n    assert analyze_orders(age=18, items=5, total=100) == \"accepted\"\n    assert analyze_orders(age=19, items=5, total=100) == \"needs_review\"\n```", "duration_sec": 55.9445}
